## The Bug

The problem is a classic **closure over a loop variable** issue. In Go 1.21, the loop variable `i` is a **single variable** that gets reused across iterations. Your goroutines capture `i` by reference (the closure captures the variable, not its current value). By the time any goroutine actually executes, the `for` loop has already finished and `i` holds the value `5`. All five goroutines therefore print `5`.

The offending line is:

```go
go func() {
    defer wg.Done()
    fmt.Println(i)  // captures the shared loop variable, not a snapshot of i
}()
```

## The Fix

Pass `i` as an argument to the goroutine function. This creates a new parameter variable scoped to that single invocation, effectively capturing the value at the time of the call:

```go
go func(j int) {
    defer wg.Done()
    fmt.Println(j)
}(i)
```

<solution language="go" verdict="fix">
package main

import (
    "fmt"
    "sync"
)

func main() {
    var wg sync.WaitGroup
    for i := 0; i < 5; i++ {
        wg.Add(1)
        go func(j int) {
            defer wg.Done()
            fmt.Println(j)
        }(i)
    }
    wg.Wait()
}
</solution>

> **Note:** If you ever upgrade to Go 1.22+, this specific bug goes away because the language spec was changed so that each loop iteration gets its own `i`. But since you're on 1.21, the explicit parameter is the correct fix.

